00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:13.460
On November 3rd, 2020, hundreds of millions
of Americans will all make their way to their

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:18.410
local polling stations to cast their votes—deciding
who will be the next President of the United

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.170
States of America.

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:25.220
That day will be the cumulation of a multi-year,
multi-billion dollar election process, but

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:30.650
before that all truly ends, the votes need
to be counted and the winner has to be declared.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:35.469
As soon as the first polls close at 6:00 PM
eastern time, there begins a massive overnight

00:00:35.469 --> 00:00:40.140
exercise to count hundreds of millions of
votes and, typically, declare who will next

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.089
lead the country by the early hours of the
following morning.

00:00:43.089 --> 00:00:47.229
While the true effort doesn’t start until
the early evening of voting day, the first

00:00:47.229 --> 00:00:51.799
results come in the early morning of election
day, before most polls have even opened.

00:00:51.799 --> 00:00:56.690
A few small towns on the East Coast—most
famously Dixville Notch, New Hampshire—all

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:00.249
compete to be the first precinct in the US
to report results.

00:01:00.249 --> 00:01:04.800
New Hampshire state law allows polling places
in the state to close early as long as all

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.440
registered voters have cast their ballots.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.370
Given that the town only has a dozen or so
registered voters, this process is typically

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.810
finished in minutes or seconds.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:18.130
In front of a cluster of cameras from nearly
every major news organization, the votes are

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:19.750
read out and tallied.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:24.020
The process of counting a dozen or so paper
ballots in a small town is not tough, but

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.920
a few hours later, the rest of the country’s
polling locations will open—each of which

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.670
eventually has to accurately count up to thousands
of votes.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:38.090
What makes the US election so difficult to
conduct right is that there are over 178,000

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:42.380
individual voting precincts, each of which
can and does do things in a slightly different

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:43.380
way.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.980
Some use paper ballots that have to be read
and interpreted manually, some use paper ballots

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:51.830
that are scanned by a machine, some use electronic
voting machines, and some even allow for absentee

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:53.560
voting over the internet.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:57.740
The selection of a voting method is a difficult
balancing act—hand-written paper ballots

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:02.030
are simple and cheap which means that a polling
station can process loads of voters at once,

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:04.950
but they’re much more difficult to count.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:08.350
Electronic voting machines are expensive and
complicated meaning they’re often in short

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:13.879
supply, leading to longer lines, but counting
and reporting the votes happens almost instantaneously.

00:02:13.879 --> 00:02:17.329
Also a major concern in the selection of voting
method is security.

00:02:17.329 --> 00:02:22.409
The most secure ballot is no doubt a handwritten
paper one, as submitting a fraudulent vote

00:02:22.409 --> 00:02:26.049
involves physically acquiring and submitting
a ballot—something that can’t be easily

00:02:26.049 --> 00:02:27.709
done at a large scale.

00:02:27.709 --> 00:02:32.740
Arguably the least secure method is electronic
voting machines as anything electronic can

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.200
be hacked.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:38.129
Almost every voting machine out there has
been hacked in some way or another in controlled

00:02:38.129 --> 00:02:40.590
experiments at hacker conventions such as
Defcon.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:44.829
In the real world, it’s impossible to know
for sure how often voting security has been

00:02:44.829 --> 00:02:50.069
compromised, but we do know that these machines
can be hacked which is why still today much

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:55.010
of the US votes the same way it did hundreds
of years ago—on paper ballots.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:59.519
First to close their polling places will be
parts of Indiana and Kentucky at 6:00 PM eastern.

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:03.170
The second that happens, everything shifts
into counting mode.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:08.049
Both states use a mix of optical scan and
direct record electronic voting machines.

00:03:08.049 --> 00:03:12.909
For example, the majority of Kentucky’s
machines are Hart InterCivic eScan’s.

00:03:12.909 --> 00:03:17.010
These are those optical scan electronic machines
where a voter physically fills out a paper

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:21.159
ballot, shading in bubbles for their preferred
candidates, then feeds the sheet into a machine

00:03:21.159 --> 00:03:25.159
which scans the responses, and then records
and tallies the votes.

00:03:25.159 --> 00:03:29.059
In Indiana, however, the majority of their
machines are Microvote Infinity’s, which

00:03:29.059 --> 00:03:32.049
are examples of direct record electronic machines.

00:03:32.049 --> 00:03:35.870
These are fully electronic machines where
the voter selects their candidates of choice

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:39.300
on the screen before pressing a button to
cast their votes.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.719
Once 6:00 PM rolls around, though, the machines’
voting modes will be turned off, they will

00:03:42.719 --> 00:03:46.720
print out a physical paper backup displaying
the tally of votes for each candidate, and

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.749
the poll workers will remove a sort of memory
device that stores the totals.

00:03:50.749 --> 00:03:55.609
Every step of this process has to be witnessed
by multiple people, usually of different political

00:03:55.609 --> 00:03:56.669
parties.

00:03:56.669 --> 00:04:00.769
These papers and memory devices are all then
packed up in sealed envelopes and gathered

00:04:00.769 --> 00:04:01.859
together.

00:04:01.859 --> 00:04:05.469
From there, the votes need to make it to a
central vote-counting location.

00:04:05.469 --> 00:04:10.420
Usually, they’re driven there by the county’s
police to assure there is no interference.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:12.989
That works in most places, but not all.

00:04:12.989 --> 00:04:17.560
For example, anyone who’s been in Los Angeles
in the early evening, when their polls close,

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:21.550
knows that it would take hours to get a vehicle
from Gorman, on the northern end of Los Angeles

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:24.350
County, to Norwalk, where votes are tallied.

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:28.530
Therefore, the county uses helicopters to
fly voting records to Norwalk from across

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:32.880
the county, and therefore ends up spending
many millions of dollars on each election

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:33.880
day.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:38.800
Traffic is less of a concern in Indiana and
Kentucky, though, and so, not long after 6:00

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.040
PM eastern, votes will start arriving at their
central counting locations where all the memory

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.850
devices will be plugged into a tabulating
computer that will record the results.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:50.700
They’ll also add in the results from early
and absentee voting as well.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:54.100
Given the electronic nature of this count,
once votes have physically arrived at the

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:58.060
site, the process doesn’t take long, but
throughout it all, it will be observed by

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:00.850
a bipartisan grouping of poll workers and
officials.

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:05.750
Of course, the way the world hears about the
results of the election is not via the government

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:08.880
itself, but rather through the media.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:12.820
Almost always, the media tells the public
who will win the election before all the votes

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:17.360
have come in, and they make this call based
off two key pieces of information.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:19.710
The first is election-day polling.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.010
There are two competing systems of polling.

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:26.410
The first is the Associated Press’ VoteCast
system—a relatively new method where voters

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:30.580
are polled at home via the internet and phone
in the days leading up to election day—and

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:36.480
the second is the National Election Pool system
used by ABC, CBS, CNN, and NBC.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.270
This system uses the more traditional exit-poll
method where individuals leaving polling stations

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:45.480
are asked who they voted for, in an attempt
to gather a representative sample.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:50.070
Both of these systems give media organizations
an idea of how voters are leaning on election

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:53.340
day, and help them know where the results
are likely to swing.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:58.650
The second major data-point used to project
election results are the results themselves.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:03.500
A small sampling of results from a county
should be roughly representative of the county’s

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:04.520
results overall.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:09.050
The Associated Press stations about 4,000
of its reporters in vote-counting centers

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:13.200
all across the country, and as soon as these
counties report results, the reporters call

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.410
them in to the AP’s office in Spokane, Washington.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:20.540
There, one of hundreds of data entry personnel
will answer the phone and put the information

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:22.360
into the AP’s system.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:26.690
These data, which may at the time of entry
be unofficial, are used by news organizations

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:28.630
all around the world.

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:31.820
Throughout the rest of the night, results
will come in precinct by precinct and the

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:34.990
AP will call the results precinct by precinct.

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:39.880
The last polls close at 1:00 AM eastern time
in Alaska, however, more than likely, the

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:43.650
country will already know who has won the
election by that time.

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:47.740
The AP’s DC bureau is in charge of calling
the race.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:51.450
They’ll one-by-one declare each state won
for one candidate or the other.

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:55.240
Some states they’ll know from the second
polls close which way they’ll go.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:07:00.680
For example, in 2016, the highly democratic
states of Massachusetts, New Jersey, Delaware,

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:04.950
and Maryland along with the District of Columbia
were all called for Clinton by the AP the

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:08.550
very minute polls closed in those places at
8:00 pm eastern.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:12.670
The AP just knew off of historical voting
trends and exit polling that they would certainly

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:14.040
go blue.

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:18.831
Meanwhile, battleground states, with a fairly
even mix of democrats and republicans, tend

00:07:18.831 --> 00:07:23.440
to be trickier to call and require a good
proportion of actual results to come in.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:29.070
For example, Minnesota’s results in 2016
weren’t called until 11:09 AM eastern and

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:33.430
Alaska’s not until 11:58 AM eastern on the
day after the election.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:38.010
Far before the last state is called, through,
the combination of electoral votes designated

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:43.500
to the called states will exceed the required
majority, 270, and at that time, the AP will

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:45.690
send out what they call a, “flash.”

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:50.710
These are the very highest tier of AP alerts,
reserved for, as they describe it, events

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:53.860
they, “expect to be one of the very top
stories of the year.”

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:58.780
The AP averages less than two flashes a year,
and such an alert has been used for events

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:03.360
like the killing of Osama Bin Laden, the resignation
of Pope Benedict, and the death of Nelson

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:04.360
Mandela.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:08.840
It is not always the AP who is the first to
call the race, but more often than not, they

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:12.170
are the first major news organization to declare
a winner.

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:16.850
Historically, this tends to happen between
9:00 and 11:00 pm eastern, but sometimes much

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:18.900
later, and sometimes much earlier.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:22.710
From there, tradition kicks in and the loser
calls the winner, the winner throws a party,

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:26.260
and the next day the sitting President calls
the President elect.

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:30.360
The true, official results might not come
in for days or weeks as the states work to

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:33.330
tally every vote—not reliant on probability.

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:38.110
The moment that happens, though, the clocks
reset and the US and world again begins the

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:43.500
four-year process leading up to the election
of the next American president.

